🔥 Main Types of Operations in Collectors
-------------------------------------------------
🔹 1. Grouping (MOST IMPORTANT 🔥):
Collectors.groupingBy()
👉 Used for:
group by gender
group by department

🔹 2. Partitioning
Collectors.partitioningBy()
👉 Splits into 2 groups (true/false)
Example:
salary > 50000 vs ≤ 50000

🔹 3. Aggregation Operations
✔ Counting
Collectors.counting()

✔ Summing
Collectors.summingDouble()

✔ Averaging
Collectors.averagingDouble()

✔ Max / Min
Collectors.maxBy()
Collectors.minBy()

🔹 4. Mapping / Transforming
Collectors.mapping()
👉 Convert data while collecting

🔹 5. Collecting to List / Set / Map
Collectors.toList()
Collectors.toSet()
Collectors.toMap()
-----------------------------------------
toMap() Example:
-----------------------------------------
Convert list to map (id → name)

Map<Integer, String> result =
    employees.stream()
             .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                 Employee::getId,
                 Employee::getName
             ));

🎯 When to use:
When converting stream into specific collection type

🔹 6. Joining (for Strings)
Collectors.joining()
🧠 6. Joining (joining)
❓ Use Case:
Join all employee names into one string
String result =
    employees.stream()
             .map(Employee::getName)
             .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));

🎯 Output:
"Amit, Rahul, Sneha"
🎯 When to use:
When you need to combine strings
-----------------------------------------------
✅ When map() is NOT needed

Only when stream already contains Strings:
List<String> names = List.of("A", "B", "C");

String result =
    names.stream()
         .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));

👉 Here:
Already Stream<String>
No need for map ✅


We use map() before joining() to transform the stream elements into strings,
 because joining() operates on a Stream<String>. If the stream already contains
  strings, mapping is not required.